{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++17 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++17 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++17 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include \"bits/stdc++.h\"\nusing namespace std;\n#define MOD 1000000007\n#define inf 0x3f3f3f3f3f3f3f3f\n#define pi acos(-1.0)\n#define ri register int\n#define int long long\n#define all(x) x.begin(), x.end()\n#ifdef ONLINE_JUDGE\nconst int maxn = 3e5 + 10;\n#else\nconst int maxn = 1e3 + 10;\n#endif\n//__builtin_popcount\nchar str[maxn];\nint N;\nsigned main() {\n#ifdef ONLINE_JUDGE\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(0);\n#endif\n    // freopen(\"C:\\Users\\hjw\\Downloads\\testdata (4).in\", \"r\", stdin);\n    cin >> str + 1;\n    N = strlen(str + 1);\n    vector<vector<string>> dp(N + 1);\n    dp[0].push_back(\"\");\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {\n        if (i >= 2 && str[i - 1] == 'a' && str[i] == 't') {\n            for (auto e : dp[i - 2]) {\n                if (e.empty() || e.find('@') != e.npos) {\n                    continue;\n                }\n                dp[i].push_back(e + \"@\");\n            }\n\n        } else if (i >= 3 && str[i - 2] == 'd' && str[i - 1] == 'o' &&\n                   str[i] == 't') {\n            for (auto e : dp[i - 3]) {\n                if (e.empty()) {\n                    continue;\n                }\n                dp[i].push_back(e + \".\");\n            }\n        }\n        for (auto e : dp[i - 1]) {\n            dp[i].push_back(e + str[i]);\n        }\n    }\n    vector<string> ans;\n    for (auto e : dp[N]) {\n        if (e.empty())\n            continue;\n        if (e.find('@') != e.npos && e.back() != '@' && e.back() != '.') {\n            ans.push_back(e);\n        }\n    }\n    sort(all(ans));\n    cout << ans.front() << endl;\n\n#ifndef ONLINE_JUDGE\n    system(\"pause\");\n#endif\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include \"bits/stdc++.h\"\nusing namespace std;\n\nconst int maxn = 3e5 + 10;\nchar str[maxn];\nint N;\n\nstring solve() {\n    cin >> str + 1;\n    N = strlen(str + 1);\n    \n    // dp[i] = set of valid prefixes of length i\n    vector<set<string>> dp(N + 1);\n    dp[0].insert(\"\");\n    \n    for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {\n        // Option 1: add current character as a letter\n        for (const string& s : dp[i-1]) {\n            dp[i].insert(s + str[i]);\n        }\n        \n        // Option 2: check for \"at\" -> '@'\n        if (i >= 2 && str[i-1] == 'a' && str[i] == 't') {\n            for (const string& s : dp[i-2]) {\n                // '@' cannot be first or last, and we can only have one '@'\n                if (s.empty()) continue;\n                int at_count = count(s.begin(), s.end(), '@');\n                if (at_count == 0) {\n                    dp[i].insert(s + '@');\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Option 3: check for \"dot\" -> '.'\n        if (i >= 3 && str[i-2] == 'd' && str[i-1] == 'o' && str[i] == 't') {\n            for (const string& s : dp[i-3]) {\n                // '.' cannot be first or last\n                if (s.empty()) continue;\n                int at_count = count(s.begin(), s.end(), '@');\n                // We can add '.' as long as it's not at the end (we'll check final validity later)\n                dp[i].insert(s + '.');\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Filter valid answers\n    vector<string> valid;\n    for (const string& s : dp[N]) {\n        if (s.empty()) continue;\n        // Must have exactly one '@'\n        if (count(s.begin(), s.end(), '@') != 1) continue;\n        // Cannot start or end with '@' or '.'\n        if (s.front() == '@' || s.front() == '.' || s.back() == '@' || s.back() == '.') continue;\n        valid.push_back(s);\n    }\n    \n    if (valid.empty()) {\n        // Fallback: try to construct manually if DP didn't find solution\n        // This handles edge cases where our DP might miss some possibilities\n        // But per problem statement, input should always have a solution\n        return \"\";\n    }\n    \n    sort(valid.begin(), valid.end());\n    return valid[0];\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(0);\n    \n    cout << solve() << endl;\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.5\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.768370445162873e-07, -7.998623186722398e-05, -1.9073468138230965e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -5.8412379075889476e-06, -0.06201557070016861, -4.446407547220588e-05, -0.00014006110723130405, -4.029192859889008e-05, -2.682172998902388e-05, -2.3841830625315197e-06, -1.1444026313256472e-05, -1.6689286894688848e-06, -3.2186455882765586e-06, -0.6326007843017578, -0.22335581481456757, -2.169585604860913e-05, -0.3095177114009857, -1.9073468138230965e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.311301275563892e-06, -0.07026109099388123, -0.0024786246940493584, -9.536738616588991e-07, 0.0, 0.0, -8.344646857949556e-07, 0.0, -0.20141518115997314, -0.02792172133922577, -7.962863310240209e-05, -1.156323378381785e-05, -0.1426825374364853, -0.004112003371119499, -1.4662635294371285e-05, -0.0006273209000937641, -1.2139198780059814, -0.4721474051475525, -0.005221657920628786, 0.0, -7.510157047363464e-06, -0.1877933144569397, -5.1377883210079744e-05, -0.16023212671279907, -4.577531944960356e-05, 0.0, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -7.152555099310121e-07, -5.960462772236497e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, 0.0, -8.83301836438477e-05, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, 0.0, 0.0, -0.01815183460712433, -0.0005550036439672112, -9.536738616588991e-07, -0.6935617327690125, -0.0027046550530940294, -7.986990567587782e-06, -0.0006622266955673695, -0.49329376220703125, -0.054341189563274384, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.11414302885532379, -0.38821089267730713, -0.12311277538537979, -0.00017915551143232733, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -0.021263141185045242, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -0.004897977691143751, -0.0002261144545627758, -1.1444026313256472e-05, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -8.583032467868179e-06, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -1.2993727978027891e-05, 0.0, -1.108640208258